The following is the SQ/USQ program that I promised I would post.  I got
it from a friend, and he is the one responsible for it; so please mail
any inquiries to him.  His readme in part 1 is fully self explanatory.

This posting is in four parts, each clearly marked.  When you save all
four parts, catenate them together and delete this and any mail-heading
info, plus the lines "Start of Part X" and "End of Part X".  You should
then be able to uudecode the file to produce SQ.ARC, like the following:

 168 -rw-r--r--  1   ????/??      156815 Aug  4 06:56 sq.arc


This is a UUENCODED file that contains SQ.ARC.  After uudecoding, and
downloading it using a *binary* protocol, you should be able to use
ARC (v. 5.10) to extract the following files:

--w   7168   3 Oct  8:06p lu.doc      
--w  22528   7 Jun  1985  lu.exe      
--w   3290   3 Apr  1985  read_me.sq  
--w   3072  29 Jul  1985  read_me.sq2 
--w  13988  10 Jun  1985  sq.exe      
--w  19968  15 Sep 10:00a sq2.exe     
--w  39040   8 Jun  1985  sq2.lbr     
--w  10002  23 May  1985  usq.exe     
--w  12398  20 Jun  1985  usq2.c      
--w  13390  10 Jun  1985  usq2.exe    
--w  61312   7 Jun  1985  usq2.lbr    

The LU program is required to unarchive the .LBR files; each .LBR file
is exactly as it was downloaded from a BBS.  The readme's and programs
themselves are either compiled from the sources, or downloaded directly
from BBSs.

The two versions of the program SQ and SQ2 are not compatible, and must
be used with their respective counterparts, USQ and USQ2.  You will know
this if one version is run on a *.?q? file, and it comes back "not a
SQueezed file".  Both versions use the same message (a little confusing,
eh?)  The read_me's should explain each one.

You should, after successfully unarchiving the ARC file, be able to use
LU on SQ2.LBR and be able to extract:

                      512        0
README               3072        4
MAKESQ                256       28
MAKEUSQ               128       30
SQ.1                 1664       31
SQ.C                 5632       44
SQ.H                 1920       88
SQDEBUG.C             896      103
SQIO.C                896      110
TR1.C                1408      117
TR2.C               13440      128
USQ.C                6528      233
USQ.H                 384      284
UTR.C                1792      287
SQCOM.H               512      301
                    39040 total bytes
15 active files, 0 deleted files, 1 empty slots.


You should also be able to use LU on USQ2.LBR and be able to extract:

                      256        0
USQ2.AQM            11392        2
USQ2.LQT            39168       91
USQ2.MQP              896      397
USQ2.OBJ             2944      404
USQ2.COM             2816      427
UTIL.H               3840      449
                    61312 total bytes
7 active files, 0 deleted files, 1 empty slots.

Note that you will have to use USQ (or USQ2) to extract the assembly
source for USQ2.  Also note, that USQ2 is preferred when squeezing
new files, because it is faster to unsqueeze, creates much smaller
files, and more efficient and popular.

All of these programs work as of the time I put this archive together.
If you have any problems with this, mail me UUCP (not arpa) at:

	....!ihnp4!pur-ee!sgh		Scott G. Hall

